            <ArticleTitle>[Emergency treatment of post-traumatic orbital emphysema: a case report].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>957-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Orbital emphysema is a common complication of orbital wall fractures (50% of blow-out fractures). It is generally a benign and self-limited condition. However, it may compromise retinal and optic nerve vascularization and therefore requires rapid management.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT AND METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">We report a case of traumatic orbital emphysema resulting in ischemic optic neuritis. The orbital emphysema was drained using a 25-gauge needle mounted on a syringe filled with normal saline, with the plunger removed, and guided by air bubble eruption.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Vision was promptly recovered following drainage of the orbital emphysema.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The development of orbital emphysema after an orbital fracture is a consequence of a forceful expiratory effort raising intranasal pressure. It must be drained, as described, in cases of vascular compression.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
